---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ALL_LOCALES } from '../i18n/ui';
import { COMPANY_NAME, SITE_URL, SUPPORT_EMAIL } from '../lib/site';

const lastUpdated = '2026-11-14';
---

<Layout
	title="Privacy Policy"
	description="Privacy Policy for the Wean Nicotine app for iPhone and Android."
	canonicalPath="/privacy/"
	availableLocales={ALL_LOCALES}
>
	<section class="page">
		<div class="container prose">
			<h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
			<p class="muted">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>

			<h2>Who we are</h2>
			<p>
				This Privacy Policy applies to the Wean Nicotine app for iPhone and Android (“Wean Nicotine”, “the app”). The data controller is{' '}
				<strong>{COMPANY_NAME}</strong>.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Website: <a href={SITE_URL}>{SITE_URL}</a></li>
				<li>
					Contact: <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Data stored on your device</h2>
			<p>
				Wean Nicotine is local‑first. The app stores your information on your phone (and does not require an
				account).
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>Usage logs (e.g., “Used a pouch”, “Resisted craving”, with timestamps)</li>
				<li>
					Settings (baseline pouches/day, reduction plan parameters, optional triggers, optional price and
					currency)
				</li>
				<li>Preferences (theme, notification toggles and times)</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>What we do not do</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>No accounts or login</li>
				<li>No ad tracking</li>
				<li>No selling personal data</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>Notifications</h2>
			<p>
				If you enable notifications, Wean Nicotine uses local notifications on your device (subject to the
				notification permission on your phone) for features like daily check‑ins and reminders.
			</p>

			<h2>Third‑party services</h2>
			<p>
				If enabled in the build, Wean Nicotine uses <strong>Sentry</strong> for crash and error reporting to help us
				improve app stability. Sentry receives exception messages, stack traces, and basic device/app context
				(app version, device model, OS version) plus screen-transition timing for performance monitoring. It also
				records when each app session starts and ends, so we can see how often the app runs without crashing.
				Your pouch logs, plan settings, baseline, price, currency, and triggers are <em>never</em> sent to Sentry.
				This is enforced both by how we call Sentry and by an automatic scrubber that strips known sensitive
				fields before transmission. We do not use Sentry for advertising.
			</p>
			<p>
				Sentry's software in the app also gives each installation a random ID and sends it with crash reports and
				sessions. It tells us whether a problem happens on one phone or on many. The ID is created by the app on
				your phone and isn't tied to your name, an email address or an account. See
				<a href="https://sentry.io/privacy/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Sentry's privacy policy</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				When the app starts, it asks <strong>Expo</strong>'s update service (<code>u.expo.dev</code>) whether a newer
				version of the app's code is available. That's how a small fix can reach you without a new App Store or
				Google Play release. The request says which platform, app version and update the app is running, and it
				carries a random ID the app creates for this (Expo calls it the EAS client ID). If the app failed to start
				the last time, a short technical error message goes along. Nothing from your logs, plan, price or triggers
				is part of the request. See
				<a href="https://expo.dev/privacy" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Expo's privacy policy</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Like any server on the internet, Sentry and Expo see the IP address a request comes from. The app doesn't
				put it in a report, and what each service keeps is covered by its privacy policy.
			</p>

			<h2>Website analytics</h2>
			<p>
				This website (<a href={SITE_URL}>{SITE_URL}</a>) uses <strong>Umami</strong>, a privacy‑friendly
				web analytics tool, to understand which pages are visited, which referrers send traffic, and which download links get clicked. It is
				loaded on every page of this site only, never inside the app.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li><strong>No cookies.</strong> Umami does not store any cookie or local-storage entry on your device.</li>
				<li><strong>No personal data.</strong> No IP addresses, fingerprints, or unique IDs are collected. Page-view counts, referrer hostnames and download-link clicks are recorded in aggregate.</li>
				<li><strong>What a click records.</strong> Clicking a link to the App Store sends the event name, which store the link points to, where on the page it sat (one of: header, hero, mid-page, footer, in-article) and the page's language. All four are fixed values chosen when the site is built. Nothing is read from you or your device.</li>
				<li><strong>No cross-site tracking.</strong> Umami cannot follow you to other sites and does not feed any ad network.</li>
				<li><strong>No GDPR consent banner.</strong> Because nothing personal is collected and no cookie is set, no consent is required under GDPR / ePrivacy. We deliberately avoid the cookie wall.</li>
				<li><strong>Self-hosted instance</strong> at <a href="https://umami-iamjarl.vercel.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">umami-iamjarl.vercel.app</a>. No third party receives the data.</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Links to the App Store also carry a campaign tag (<code>ct=site</code>) that is the same for every visitor. It lets
				Apple's App Store Connect show us, in aggregate, how many App Store visits and downloads started from this website.
				The tag is fixed when the site is built and says nothing about you. What the App Store itself records is covered by
				Apple's privacy policy.
			</p>

			<h2>Data retention</h2>
			<p>
				Your data stays on your device unless you delete the app or use “Start Over” inside the app to
				delete local data and return to onboarding.
			</p>

			<h2>Your choices</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>Delete data: use “Start Over” inside the app, or delete the app.</li>
				<li>
					Contact us: email <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>

<style>
	.page {
		padding: clamp(44px, 6vw, 84px) 0;
	}

	.prose {
		max-width: 76ch;
	}

	h1 {
		margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
		font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 44px);
		line-height: 1.1;
		letter-spacing: -0.03em;
	}

	h2 {
		margin: var(--space-xxl) 0 var(--space-sm);
		font-size: 20px;
		letter-spacing: -0.01em;
	}

	p,
	li {
		color: var(--text-secondary);
	}

	.muted {
		margin: 0 0 var(--space-xl);
		color: var(--text-tertiary);
	}

	ul {
		margin: var(--space-sm) 0 0;
		padding-left: 18px;
	}

	li {
		margin: 6px 0;
	}
</style>

